\" Generated by script2man from auto-enable-service
.TH auto-enable-service 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-enable-service - Enable and start a service

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-enable-service [-s script-path] service-name caller|nocomment
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
-s script-path  Specify the full pathname of the service script
service-name    Name of the service as specified to the native command
caller          Name of the calling program, usually $0, or "nocomment"
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-enable-service
command enables and starts a service.  It is primarily a compatibility layer
to mask the extensive differences in how services are handled
between various Unix platforms such as BSD, Linux, and macOS.
This makes it possible to write portable code that manipulate
services, assuming the service has the same name across platforms,
as is often the case.

Note: The service name used here is the name of the service
as specified to underlying native commands such as "service"
or "systemctl".

If the service was not already listed in the configuration file,
an entry is added.
If caller is not "nocomment", the new entry is wrapped in a
comment as follows:

.nf
.na
# Added by auto-admin from $caller
service_enable=YES
# End $caller addition
.ad
.fi

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-enable-service lpjs_compd nocomment
auto-enable-service lpjs_compd $0
auto-enable-service -s /usr/pkg/share/lpjs/Systemd/lpjs_compd.service lpjs_compd nocomment
auto-enable-service -s /usr/pkg/share/lpjs/Launchd/org.pkgsrc.lpjs_compd.plist org.pkgsrc.lpjs_compd nocomment
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-disable-service(8), auto-service-enabled(8),
auto-restart-services(8), auto-services-manager(8)

